{
  "ID": "9.2",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Look at the differential genes between high and low grade IPMN and between high grade IPMN and ND. From the commonly upregulated proteins, nominate a few important genes that are related to Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) in the literature.  ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-15 18:59:32",
  "elapsed_seconds": 489.55,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nWang et al. profiled 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs) and 76 grossly normal duct tissues (ND) by proteomics/glycoproteomics to identify proteins differentially expressed in high-grade (HG) versus low-grade (LG) IPMN, and HG IPMN versus ND. 7624 proteins were quantified; 279 showed concordant upregulation in HG versus both LG IPMN and ND (fold-change > 0, FDR < 0.05).  Details on sample replicates and instrumentation are not specified.\n\nKeywords  \n• IPMN progression  \n• High-grade IPMN  \n• Proteomics biomarker  \n• PDAC relevance  \n• Glutamine/iron transport  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nFrom the 279 common upregulated proteins, we highlighted five with mechanistic ties to pancreatic cancer biology:  \n• SLC1A5 (ASCT2)  \n• CDCP1  \n• TFRC (CD71)  \n• LGALS9 (galectin-9)  \n• MCM3  \n\nData‐driven prioritization  \n1. csv_read of the full Table S2M revealed 7624 rows (columns: “Gene,” “HG vs LG IPMN,” FDR, “HG vs ND,” FDR.1).  \n2. csv_filter retained genes with positive fold-changes and FDR < 0.05 in both contrasts (279 genes).  \n3. csv_filter/csv_select ranking by fold-change highlighted top hits (FCGBP, TFF3, FAM3D, CDH17, PDILT, SLC1A5, …).  \n4. Union of literature‐known PDAC players with top 279 yielded the five candidate genes above.\n\nNomination of PDAC-related Genes  \n• SLC1A5 (ASCT2): A sodium-dependent neutral amino acid/glutamine transporter essential for PDAC growth and survival via mTORC1 signaling.  \n• CDCP1: A transmembrane CUB-domain protein phosphorylated by Src family kinases; promotes PDAC cell migration and metastasis.  \n• TFRC (CD71): Transferrin receptor mediating iron uptake; upregulated in PDAC, supports proliferation and survival under metabolic stress.  \n• LGALS9 (Galectin-9): A lectin modulating immune cell interactions; implicated in PDAC immune evasion.  \n• MCM3: A minichromosome maintenance protein required for DNA replication licensing; overexpressed in PDAC correlating with aggressive tumor behavior.  \n\nNext Steps & Clarification  \n1. Validate expression of the five genes by IHC in independent HG IPMN and PDAC cohorts.  \n2. Correlate SLC1A5 and TFRC levels with metabolic flux (glutamine uptake, iron uptake) in IPMN‐derived organoids.  \n3. Assess CDCP1 and LGALS9 functional roles in IPMN to PDAC progression using knockdown/overexpression.  \n4. Examine MCM3 as a proliferation marker in IPMN histological sections to refine grading criteria.\n\nReferences  \nNone validated by downstream PubMed/Rummagene queries.",
